WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
The nuclear pore is not a one-way door.

2
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.860
So how can the same pore deliver one protein inward and another outward?

3
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.060
Watch one pore between the cytoplasm and nucleus.

4
00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:19.550
Soluble cargo floats within either compartment instead of sitting in a membrane.

5
00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.090
Here is a first picture.

6
00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:28.850
For soluble protein cargo, the nuclear pore is a selective passage, not a one-way

7
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:33.630
motor. Small grey particles slip between moving teal chains.

8
00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:37.270
Larger yellow cargo crosses slowly alone.

9
00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.270
Receptors engage the barrier for faster passage.

10
00:00:42.383 --> 00:00:46.483
Cargo carries labels that transport-receptor proteins can read.

11
00:00:47.803 --> 00:00:52.023
A blue NLS tag recruits blue importin α, which holds cargo.

12
00:00:53.883 --> 00:00:56.163
Importin β joins it and engages the pore.

13
00:00:57.483 --> 00:01:02.543
An orange NES tag can recruit the purple export receptor CRM1.

14
00:01:03.583 --> 00:01:08.103
The difference between the two sides comes from Ran, a small switch protein.

15
00:01:09.643 --> 00:01:12.243
Ran is on with GTP and off with GDP.

16
00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:16.703
Green RanGTP is abundant in the nucleus.

17
00:01:18.263 --> 00:01:20.883
Grey RanGDP dominates in the cytoplasm.

18
00:01:22.123 --> 00:01:25.103
This unequal distribution is the Ran gradient.

19
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:30.147
In the simplest form, the import complex threads through the pore.

20
00:01:31.347 --> 00:01:35.207
It moves from cytoplasm to nucleus without a directional push.

21
00:01:36.587 --> 00:01:43.667
In the nucleus, green RanGTP binds importin β and separates it from importin α.

22
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:49.327
Importin α then releases the blue-tagged cargo where RanGTP is high.

23
00:01:49.327 --> 00:01:53.587
All three receptor and cargo pieces are now distinct.

24
00:01:54.750 --> 00:02:01.930
Now predict. If RanGTP became equally common on both sides, could importin still

25
00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:03.250
unload only inside?

26
00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.590
Hold the two-sided picture in your mind.

27
00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:15.513
No. Importin could still cross.

28
00:02:16.713 --> 00:02:20.333
But on either side, it might drop cargo or fail to pick it up.

29
00:02:21.713 --> 00:02:23.833
Net import would lose its one-sided rule.

30
00:02:25.413 --> 00:02:29.213
Restore the gradient, and cargo release again favors the nuclear side.

31
00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:32.407
Export reverses the same rule.

32
00:02:34.007 --> 00:02:39.687
Nuclear green RanGTP helps purple CRM1 grip orange-tagged NES cargo.

33
00:02:41.047 --> 00:02:42.547
The complex crosses outward.

34
00:02:43.847 --> 00:02:48.267
In the cytoplasm, Ran-binding proteins first help the cargo come free.

35
00:02:49.867 --> 00:02:53.027
Hydrolysis then turns RanGTP into RanGDP.

36
00:02:54.447 --> 00:02:56.727
This frees CRM1 for another round.

37
00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:03.250
Nuclear RanGTP tells importins to unload and exportins to load.

38
00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.050
Blue importins carry NLS cargo in.

39
00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:11.910
Purple CRM1 carries many NES cargoes out.

40
00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:16.630
One asymmetry gives opposite receptors useful directions.

41
00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:24.270
In Ran-dependent protein transport, GTP hydrolysis maintains the gradient; it does

42
00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.850
not push cargo through the pore.

43
00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:31.670
In Ran-dependent transport, Ran mainly sets where cargo loads and unloads.

44
00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:37.890
Pore proteins still help on each face, but the pore sets no direction of its own.

45
00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:42.710
Most messenger RNA export uses another receptor.
